# Plugin pack for the `agency` profile.
#
# One plugin per line, `slug` or `slug=version`. Slugs are wordpress.org slugs,
# exactly what `wp plugin install` takes. Lines starting with # are ignored.
#
# All of these are free. Elementor Pro and other paid plugins are deliberately
# absent: a fixture nobody else can rebuild is not a benchmark.
#
# On pinning: leaving a plugin unpinned installs the current release, which is
# reproducible *today* but not next year. Whatever you use, the manifest records
# the exact versions that were installed, so a result can always state what it
# ran against. To pin, copy the versions out of a manifest into this file.

# The page builder itself, plus the two add-ons almost every Elementor site has.
# Between them they register a lot of hooks and widgets — that is the point.
elementor
header-footer-elementor
essential-addons-for-elementor-lite

# SEO: rewrites titles and injects meta on every request, one of the heaviest
# per-request costs on a typical site.
wordpress-seo

# Forms, mail and redirects — unremarkable, ubiquitous, and each one hooks init.
contact-form-7
wp-mail-smtp
redirection

# Backup plugin: the classic "dead weight" that loads on every request and does
# nothing for the visitor.
updraftplus

# Wordfence is the other classic, and it is genuinely heavy — but it is left out
# on purpose: its firewall counts requests and will start rate-limiting or
# blocking the load generator mid-run, which silently corrupts the numbers.
# Enable it only for a scenario specifically about security-plugin overhead, and
# whitelist the runner first.
# wordfence

# No caching plugin here, by design: the fixture must measure the server stack,
# not a WordPress cache. See BENCHMARK-PLAN.md §7 for the variant that adds one.
